**New evidence**
- Scholz’s cabinet took office after his 395-vote Bundestag election, which establishes the 2022 executive baseline and his appointment authority.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Federal Government in office","publisher":"Federal Government","publication_date":"2021-12-08","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-en/service/archive/federal-chancellor-election-1989862","claim":"Olaf Scholz was elected Federal Chancellor with 395 votes and the new federal ministers were appointed and sworn in the same day.","locator":"archive article body and photo captions","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal-government record of the 2021 government transition.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline immediately before the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Baseline context for Scholz's 2022 authority, not a 2022 economic policy act itself.","contrary_evidence":["This is a 2021 transition record, not an outcome measure for 2022.","It establishes office and appointment authority rather than economic competence."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.10"]}
- The 2022 Meseberg retreat shows Scholz working directly with Habeck and Lindner on energy security and supply, which is the strongest available appointment/empowerment evidence in the year.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Closed Cabinet meeting at Schloss Meseberg","publisher":"Federal Government","publication_date":"2022-08-30","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-en/service/archive/kabinettklausur-meseberg-2082168","claim":"Scholz said the cabinet discussed energy security and energy supply with Economic Affairs Minister Robert Habeck and Finance Minister Christian Lindner in a constructive atmosphere.","locator":"concluding press conference caption and quoted summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct federal-government archive with named participants and contemporaneous summary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 cabinet coordination on the core economic shock.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz cabinet leadership; this shows he empowered the key economic ministers.","contrary_evidence":["Constructive coordination is not the same as proven policy success.","It is an elite-level meeting, not evidence of household-level impact."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7"]}
- The minimum wage rise to 12 euro is the clearest pro-work, pro-low-income policy of the year.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Der Mindestlohn steigt auf 12 Euro","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-06-03","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Meldungen/2022/mindestlohn-steigt-auf-12-euro.html","claim":"The Bundestag approved the government bill to raise the statutory minimum wage to 12 euro per hour from 1 October 2022, benefiting more than six million people, especially women and workers in eastern Germany.","locator":"press release body and opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry announcement of an enacted policy with direct distributional effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 policy implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-level policy under Scholz; implementation was through the federal government and coalition majority.","contrary_evidence":["A higher minimum wage can add some wage-cost pressure.","This is a targeted welfare measure, not a broad productivity reform by itself."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.8","5B.9"]}
- The government also moved quickly on child and social support, indicating concern for households under strain.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mehr Unterstützung für Familien mit wenig Geld","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-03-16","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2022/mehr-unterstuetzung-fuer-familien-mit-wenig-geld.html","claim":"The cabinet approved a child immediate supplement of 20 euro per month and a one-off payment for people on social-security benefits as an early crisis response.","locator":"press release body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary cabinet-policy announcement from the responsible ministry.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Early 2022 crisis response.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-level policy under Scholz, aimed at low-income households.","contrary_evidence":["It is emergency relief rather than structural anti-poverty reform.","The measure is compensatory, not a permanent earnings increase."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}
- The first supplementary budget turned the early crisis response into a package of concrete household and transport relief.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BMF-Monatsbericht Mai 2022 - Der Ergänzungshaushalt: gezielt Bürgerinnen und Bürger entlasten","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-05","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Monatsberichte/2022/05/Inhalte/Kapitel-2b-Schlaglicht/2b-ergaenzungshaushalt.html","claim":"The cabinet adopted a supplementary budget to respond transparently and pragmatically to the new security and economic situation, including a fuel-tax cut, the 9-euro ticket, a one-off energy payment, a child bonus, and a higher basic allowance.","locator":"opening paragraphs and bullet list of measures","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official finance-ministry summary with a detailed list of measures and fiscal rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-policy implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Federal-government action under Scholz, with direct fiscal design by the finance ministry.","contrary_evidence":["Broad relief can be expensive and imperfectly targeted.","Some measures were temporary and did not solve underlying inflation pressures."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}
- The July package summary shows the coalition layering tax relief, transport relief, and one-off payments rather than relying on a single instrument.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BMF-Monatsbericht Juli 2022 - Leistungen der Entlastungspakete","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-07","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Monatsberichte/2022/07/Inhalte/Kapitel-2b-Schlaglicht/Tabelle/interview-entlastungspakete.html","claim":"The ministry tabulated the first and second relief packages, including the EEG surcharge abolition, fuel-tax cuts, the 9-euro ticket, the energy price lump sum, child bonus, and higher tax allowances.","locator":"table 'Leistungen der Entlastungspakete'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct ministry table documenting the actual composition of the 2022 relief measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 implementation details.","ruler_attribution":"Government policy under Scholz, implemented through the finance ministry and coalition partners.","contrary_evidence":["The same table shows these are broad compensatory measures, not structural reform.","Temporary relief does not guarantee durable prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}
- The third package shows the coalition trying to preserve energy-saving incentives while continuing broad support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BMF-Monatsbericht September 2022 - Wuchtige Unterstützung: das Entlastungspaket III","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-09","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Monatsberichte/2022/09/Inhalte/Kapitel-2b-Schlaglicht/2b-drittes-entlastungspaket.html","claim":"The third relief package followed the first two packages of about 30 billion euro, adding further support for households and firms while explicitly aiming to preserve savings incentives amid the energy crisis.","locator":"opening paragraphs and section 'Energie: Versorgung sichern, Kosten dämpfen'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct finance-ministry crisis summary with explicit package totals and policy logic.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 energy-crisis policy.","ruler_attribution":"Federal-government policy under Scholz.","contrary_evidence":["The ministry’s own framing admits the pressure on prices would not disappear quickly.","Broader support can dilute targeting."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.7","5B.9"]}
- The 200 billion euro economic shield is the central fiscal crisis response, but it also drew later criticism about targeting and legality.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BMF-Monatsbericht Oktober 2022 - Wirtschaftlicher Abwehrschirm und Gaspreisbremse: mit Entschlossenheit durch die Energiekrise","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-10","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Monatsberichte/2022/10/Inhalte/Kapitel-2b-Schlaglicht/2b-mit-entschlossenheit-durch-die-energiekrise.html","claim":"On 29 September 2022 the government announced a 200 billion euro economic shield financed through the WSF, including gas and heating price brakes, support for firms, and energy-saving incentives, with the funds strictly limited to crisis measures.","locator":"opening paragraphs and bullet list","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry account of the flagship 2022 fiscal intervention.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 policy decision and design.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz government policy, jointly announced with the finance and economy ministries.","contrary_evidence":["Later critics argued the instrument was overly broad and legally fragile.","The package is crisis stabilization, not proof of long-run structural prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- OECD’s country survey is a strong independent assessment that the government reacted quickly, but that the relief could have been more targeted.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Germany 2023","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2023-05-08","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-germany-2023_9642a3f5-en.html","claim":"The OECD said the government acted swiftly to secure energy supply and support households and firms, while noting that the relief measures could be better targeted to limit fiscal costs and that structural reform needs remained large.","locator":"Executive summary, especially the section on the energy crisis","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major independent surveillance report with direct assessment of the German response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but directly assessing 2022 policy response and outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Scholz; this is an external evaluation rather than self-justification.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD’s own critique is that the support could have been better targeted.","It frames the 2022 response as necessary but incomplete."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The IMF reached a broadly similar view, describing the policy response as timely and generally well designed.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Germany: 2023 Article IV Consultation - Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for Germany","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2023-07-17","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2023/07/14/germany-2023-article-iv-consultation-press-release-staff-report-and-statement-by-the-536350","claim":"The IMF said Germany’s policy response to spillovers from Russia’s war in Ukraine was timely and generally well designed, and it recommended fiscal tightening to support disinflation.","locator":"summary section and executive summary","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative IMF surveillance report with explicit policy evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but focused on the 2022 shock response.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Scholz; the IMF assesses the German authorities as a whole.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also expects weak growth and continued inflation pressure.","It recommends tighter fiscal policy, which implies the 2022 stance was still not fully optimal."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Inflation and real income erosion were severe, limiting how far the government’s relief could go.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inflation rate at +7.9% in 2022","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-17","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_022_611.html","claim":"Germany’s annual average consumer-price inflation was 7.9 percent in 2022, driven mainly by energy and food price surges after the war in Ukraine, even though relief measures partly mitigated the shock.","locator":"opening paragraphs and the explanatory quote from Destatis","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national statistics source for the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; the inflation shock was largely external, but the policy response is part of the record.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation is not a pure ruler-control metric in a war and energy shock year.","Some of the price pressure came from external supply and war conditions."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Germany’s economy still expanded in 2022 despite the shock, which matters for trajectory but not as a sole success indicator.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gross domestic product up 1.9% in 2022","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-13","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_020_811.html","claim":"Real GDP rose 1.9 percent in 2022, and Destatis said the economy held up despite Ukraine-war energy-price shocks, material shortages, and lingering pandemic effects.","locator":"opening paragraph and annual-growth table","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national accounts release with explicit 2022 growth and context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed attribution: partly government policy, partly inherited resilience and external conditions.","contrary_evidence":["GDP growth alone does not show broad household well-being.","The same release stresses severe external shocks."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Households did worse than GDP headline growth suggests, because real earnings fell sharply in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nach Revision: Reallöhne im Jahr 2022 um 4,0 % gegenüber 2021 gesunken","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-04-27","url":"https://www.destatis.de/DE/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2023/04/PD23_166_62321.html","claim":"After revision, real earnings in Germany fell 4.0 percent in 2022, the largest loss since 2008, because consumer prices rose faster than nominal earnings.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary statistical release on household purchasing power.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 household-welfare outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; the drop mainly reflects inflation and shock conditions, not just domestic policy.","contrary_evidence":["Nominal earnings still rose, so the loss was inflation-driven rather than a collapse of wages.","This does not isolate the chancellor’s personal contribution."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Employment remained unusually strong, which is a genuine positive for the year’s trajectory.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Employment in 2022 at highest level since German unification","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-02","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_001_13321.html","claim":"Average employment in 2022 reached about 45.6 million people, the highest level since German unification, rising by 589,000 from 2021.","locator":"opening paragraph and headline figures","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary labor-market statistics source for the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz, influenced by earlier labor-market institutions and external conditions as well as policy.","contrary_evidence":["Employment strength does not erase the inflation and real-income losses in the same year.","Immigration and prior labor-market trends also helped."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}
- Poverty and social exclusion did not improve materially in 2022, which is a useful counterweight to the macro headline.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Just over one-fifth of Germany’s population at risk of poverty or social exclusion","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-05-16","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/05/PE23_190_63.html","claim":"In 2022, 20.9 percent of Germany’s population, about 17.3 million people, were at risk of poverty or social exclusion, nearly unchanged from 2021.","locator":"opening paragraph and bullet points","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary social-indicators release with exact 2022 distribution outcome.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 distributional outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; this is a broad social indicator, not a ruler-specific policy test.","contrary_evidence":["Nearly unchanged poverty risk means the year was not a broad distributional breakthrough.","The metric does not isolate the effect of the chancellor alone."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
- The federal audit office later criticized the energy-shield financing as legally and fiscally questionable.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Energie-Schutzschirm: Finanzierung zweifelhaft","publisher":"Bundesrechnungshof","publication_date":"2023-08-29","url":"https://www.bundesrechnungshof.de/SharedDocs/Kurzmeldungen/DE/2023/wsf-energiekrise/wirtschaftsstabilisierungsfonds.html","claim":"The Bundesrechnungshof warned that the 200 billion euro WSF energy shield involved significant fiscal and constitutional risks and said the finance ministry tried to exhaust the 2022 borrowing authorization in a way that undercut the debt rule.","locator":"opening paragraphs and the section describing the financing construction","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent federal audit body with direct criticism of the financing design.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but focused on the 2022 design decision.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level criticism under Scholz; this concerns finance ministry design choices, not merely macro outcomes.","contrary_evidence":["This criticism is about financing design, not the purpose of relief itself.","It is retrospective and therefore less direct than contemporaneous reporting."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}
- Independent scholars also criticized the packages as very large and potentially too broad.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Entlastungspakete, Tankrabatt, Übergewinnabschöpfung: Wie weit kann der Staat uns in Krisen schützen?","publisher":"ifo Institut","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.ifo.de/publikationen/2022/aufsatz-zeitschrift/entlastungspakete-tankrabatt-uebergewinnabschopfung","claim":"ifo researchers estimated the budget effects of the relief packages at about 130 billion euro over three years and argued that broad compensation can crowd out public goods or lead to higher future taxes.","locator":"abstract and summary paragraph","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Credible applied-economics analysis, though it is a policy essay rather than a government record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses the 2022 relief strategy.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level policy critique under Scholz.","contrary_evidence":["The paper critiques scope and financing, not the entire crisis response.","Its estimates are analytical, not a final fiscal audit."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 distinct documents across the Federal Government archive, BMAS, BMF, Destatis, OECD, IMF, Bundesrechnungshof, ifo, Bundestag, and a small amount of supporting archive material.
- I kept the strongest non-duplicative evidence on cabinet formation, economic-team coordination, wages, household relief, the energy shield, inflation, GDP, employment, real earnings, poverty, and external assessments.
- I rejected older pre-2022 items unless they were needed for baseline or appointment context.
- I rejected most generic government pages on growth, sustainability, or social policy when they did not add a distinct 2022 claim.
- I rejected purely descriptive archive pages that repeated the same relief measures without adding a new material point.
- I rejected several pages about later 2023-2026 reforms because they were out of period or only restated the same policy baseline.
- I rejected niche tax or labor-market pages that did not clearly connect to Scholz-era 2022 economic choices.
- I rejected broad macro indicators unless they helped distinguish policy effects from the external shock environment.

**Remaining gaps**
- 5B.2 is still only moderately evidenced: I have cabinet formation and clear coordination around the energy shock, but not a direct external assessment of Scholz’s appointment choices as such.
- 5B.5 remains thin. I did not find strong, ruler-specific evidence of corruption, favoritism, or oligarchic capture in 2022, which is itself informative but still a gap.
- Household distribution data are decent but not perfect. I have poverty risk, real earnings, and the minimum wage, but not a full 2022 household-incidence model for every relief measure.
- The year is dominated by external shocks, especially the Ukraine war and energy prices, so attribution must stay modest. GDP and employment were resilient, but real wages and inflation show the cost to households.
- More searching would help mainly if you want a narrower deep dive on who benefited most from the relief packages, or a more explicit appraisal of finance-ministry design choices and debt-brake compliance.

